# The merchant's tune-up (ss_shop.cpp LvUpConfirm::move) wrote the four weapon levels through
# TuneLevel, a structure of four 4-bit bitfields laid over cItem::lv (u16). The rest of the game
# reads the levels as nibbles of the value (fire << 12 | firing speed << 8 | reload << 4 |
# capacity: LvUpItemSelect, LvUpConfirm::init, ItemMgr). Big-endian compilers give the first
# bitfield the top bits, ARM and x86 the bottom ones: every tune-up landed on other levels
# (firepower on the capacity, firing speed on the reload speed and back), so the upgrade seemed not
# to work and a reload level could pass its table (no reload any more). Testers' reports
# 22.10.2026 (0.41.3: "upgrading the weapon did not work", "after an upgrade I could not reload").
# The levels are written as the value now, with a log line per tune-up (`shop: tune-up ...`).
# Found again by port/tools/scan/view_scan.py (`bits`). Weapons tuned before keep their mixed
# levels.
--- a/src/Sscrn/ss_shop.cpp
+++ b/src/Sscrn/ss_shop.cpp
@@ -2526,33 +2526,21 @@
     if (msg == 0x17 && (result = cMes.GetSelectMessage(1)) != 0) {
         switch (result) {
         case 1: {
-            TuneLevel* t;
-
-            t = (TuneLevel*) &sw->m_p_item->lv;
-            // COMPILER-DIFF: 12 (combine). The target keeps `extsb` before `addi -1; clrlwi 24; slwi 12`; our
-            // combine strips the sign extension under the u8 truncation when both are one expression.
+            /* PORT (patch 0094): the four levels as the nibbles the rest of the game reads (fire << 12 |
+               mag << 8 | speed << 4 | ex, item.h). The original wrote them through TuneLevel, a 4-bit
+               bitfield view of the u16: big-endian the first field is the top nibble, on ARM and x86
+               the bottom one, so a tune-up landed on another level (firepower on the capacity, the
+               firing speed on the reload, a reload level past its table: no reload any more). */
             {
-                int v = (s8) sw->m_level_no[0];
-                t->fire = (u8) (v - 1);
-            }
-            // Byte first, item pointer second in every nibble: the `lbz` between the previous `sth`
-            // and the next `lwz item` keeps local-alloc's fake lifetimes of the four item-pointer
-            // qtys apart (all r11; adjacent `sth; lwz` alternates r11/r10), and the LUID puts the
